WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:13.160
OK, so as discussed on Monday
for this lecture, actually,

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.320
we're going to go through
some of the key concepts

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.260
behind classical optimization.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.880
So nonlinear
optimization problem

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.800
where we solve over a finite
amount of decision variables.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.022
And the idea here
is not that this

00:00:29.022 --> 00:00:30.480
is a class about
optimization, this

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.860
is a class about optimal
learning-based control.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.920
But many of the insights
from classical optimization

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.280
carry over to the field of
optimal and learning-based

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.720
control.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.960
So in particular,
what we did on Monday

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.520
was to define what a nonlinear
optimization problem is.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.940
And what does it mean
for a point or a vector,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.720
I will use this terminology
interchangeably,

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.710
to be a local minimum
or a global minimum

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.670
of a given objective function f?

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.950
What we're going to do
now is a couple of things.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.750
So first, derive a basic
necessary conditions

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.190
for optimality.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.070
So these are conditions
that essentially

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:19.990
say if a given point of
vector is a local minimum,

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.270
then something has to happen.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.810
And typically, people use this
necessary optimality conditions,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.150
for example, as
filters to filter out

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.810
candidates for optimality.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.790
The fact that you fulfill a
necessary optimality condition

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.990
doesn't guarantee that, that
point is a local minimum.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.230
But if it does not fulfill,
if a point does not

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.770
fulfill that necessary
optimality condition,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.590
there is not a chance that, that
point can be a local minimum.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.910
So, how do we derive
the necessary conditions

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:50.970
for optimality?

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:55.590
So the idea is that you assume
that you are at a local minimum,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.500
and you see whether there are
any particular insights that you

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.020
can derive from the fact that
a point is a local minimum.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:07.060
Particularly, the
idea is to reason

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:14.140
in terms of what happens when we
perturb a local minimum denoted

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.780
as x star by a small
perturbation delta x.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.380
And we try to find
some general rule that

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.100
can serve as our necessary
optimality condition.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.000
Specifically, from
basic calculus,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.220
we know that if f
is differentiable,

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:38.020
f of x star plus
delta x, where delta x

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.160
is a small displacement--

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:53.140
so you're just moving a tiny bit
out of x star in some direction,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:59.080
is approximately equal to
f of x star plus gradient

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:06.220
of f evaluated at x
star times delta x.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.440
Prime means transpose.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.340
So you should be familiar
with this notion.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.880
Of course, there are
some error terms here,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.920
but the idea is that as delta
x gets closer and closer,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.120
the first order term,
so the term that

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.920
includes the derivative
becomes the dominant one.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.360
And so the other terms
can be neglected.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.560
And so roughly speaking, we
can look at this approximation

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.520
as indeed being an equality.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.800
Or one can go one step further
and consider a second order

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.700
approximation, where in addition
to the first-order term,

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.880
you also account for
a second-order term.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.010
This is the action
of the function f.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.190
So basically, the matrix that
collects the second order

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.070
derivatives.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.110
Are you OK with this?

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.010
Yeah.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.570
Are you taking gradient the
over the derivative of that

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:12.270
or is it you're taking the
gradient over the function of?

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.890
Oh, no, gradient of
f is just notation.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.930
So this is the nabla symbol.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.550
So by definition,
nabla f is defined

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:30.030
as the vector of the derivatives
with respect to each variable.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.030
Oh, yeah.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:37.624
X2 dot, dot, dot, dot,
dot derivative of xn.

00:04:37.624 --> 00:04:41.103
[INAUDIBLE]

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.270
This one?

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.230
Oh, yeah, just transpose.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.110
It could be like T
or here in this class

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:55.260
I use other transpose or
the transfers in this class

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.400
will either use the
symbol capital T or prime.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.780
Yeah, same thing here.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.140
OK.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:13.860
So, so far, nothing
particularly interesting.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:20.620
Now let's apply the fact
that we are assuming

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:25.100
that x star is a local minimum.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.060
So if x star is a local
minimum, by the definition

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:33.020
of a local minimum, if I
move around that point,

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.180
the function f has to
assume a larger value.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.260
This by definition for a
point to be a local minimum.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:48.940
Which means that f of x star
plus delta x minus f of x star

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.420
has to be greater
than or equal to 0.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.170
This is because we are
assuming that x star is

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.410
a local minimum, which means
that if we move around,

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:02.570
then the function f has
to take on a larger value.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.750
But if this is the case, and
assuming that delta x is small.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.710
So this relation can be
taken as an inequality.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.550
So this implies that the--

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:28.530
remember that f of x star
plus delta x minus f of x star

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.890
is equal to the
gradient times delta x.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:39.370
So that implies--
some worse, worse.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:50.600
This implies that gradient of f
evaluated x star times delta x

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.920
has to be greater
than or equal to 0.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.620
I write the scalar
product in expanded form.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.840
So I carry out the product.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.720
So this implies that
the summation from i

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:09.640
equals 1 to n or the partial
derivative of f with respect

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:16.060
to xi times the delta xi is
greater than or equal to 0,

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:18.920
where the delta xi are the
displacement with respect

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:20.920
to each ith coordinate.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:27.140
So delta x is a vector with
displacement delta x1, delta x2,

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:28.480
dot, dot, dot delta xn.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:33.820
Any questions about that?

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.720
So remember that if x
star is a local minimum,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.000
then f of x star
plus delta x has

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.980
to be greater than or equal to f
of x star for all possible delta

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:52.060
x small enough.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.220
That is for all
possible delta x.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.340
Let's just fix a few possible
choices for the delta x.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.260
So let's consider
displacement of the type delta

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.340
x equal to epsilon, where
epsilon is a small scalar

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.480
positive number.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.980
Say 1, 0, 0, 0.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:22.660
So this will be say delta x1
or another possible choice

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:28.180
for the vector delta x could be
delta x2 equal epsilon, where

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:31.380
all the coordinates are
0, except the second one,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.299
and so on.

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:36.960
So this is just a specific
choice of delta x.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.580
This relation has to be true
for all possible choices.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:44.059
I'm just selecting a
few possible choices.

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:46.980
But if I select
delta x in these ways

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.450
and then I carry out
this color product,

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:52.730
you'll see that all those
products are equal to 0,

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:57.770
except for the th coordinate.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.050
So if I start plugging in
all these possible choices

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.170
of displacements, I
start getting conditions

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.170
of the type epsilon
delta partial derivative

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:14.610
of f with respect to xi times
1 greater than or equal to 0.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.590
But I also and take the
negative of this one.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.450
So I get the minus
epsilon partial derivative

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:25.530
of f with respect to xi times
1 less than or equal to 0.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:27.810
Epsilon is small
positive number.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.170
So this, I can just cancel
it out from this inequality.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:37.890
So essentially, I get a bunch
of inequality of this type.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.170
So wrapping up, we have
found that if x star

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:46.320
is a local minimum, then
each partial derivative has

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.280
to be simultaneously greater
than or equal to 0 or less than

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.680
or equal to 0.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.280
So, what is the only number
that is simultaneously

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.420
greater than or equal to
0 less than or equal to 0?

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:57.400
0.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.800
So basically, we've just
proven that each partial--

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.480
if x star is a
local minimum, then

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.080
each partial derivative
of f with respect to xi

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.840
has to be equal to 0.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.180
Putting all those
results together,

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:13.680
we get to the celebrated
necessary optimality condition

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.200
that says that at
a local minimum,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:19.160
if x star is a local minimum,
then the gradient has

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:20.700
to be equal to 0.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:35.840
Pictorially, in the
one dimensional case,

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:37.260
say that you have a function.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.310
Say that you have a
function like this one.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:50.550
So this is a local minimum.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:55.590
And indeed, you see that here,
the derivative is equal to 0.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.183
Here the derivative
is equal to 0.

00:10:58.183 --> 00:10:59.850
But this is clearly
not a local minimum.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.230
This is a local maximum.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.510
So that shows that the fact
that the gradient is equal to 0

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:06.330
is a necessary condition
for optimality,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.702
but is not a sufficient
condition for optimality.

00:11:09.702 --> 00:11:11.410
And probably you
already know about this,

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.728
but my point here
is just to reiterate

00:11:13.728 --> 00:11:15.770
the concept of a necessary
optimality conditions,

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:17.145
because as I said,
it will become

00:11:17.145 --> 00:11:21.030
quite useful as we move on.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:23.570
Now, of course, we can
go one step further.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:27.070
So, so far, we have used a
first-order approximation

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:28.270
of a function.

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.870
But we can also use a
second-order approximation

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.830
of a function and see whether
the fact that a point is

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:37.510
a local minimum tells us
something about the function

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:40.710
beyond just the first
order derivative.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:46.530
Well, remember that the
second-order approximation

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.750
of a function reads
like the following.

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.730
So you have a linear term
and a quadratic term,

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:56.450
quadratic because the delta
x multiply each other.

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:58.310
So by the same
reasoning as before,

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:00.450
if x star is a local
minimum, this difference

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.970
has to be greater
than or equal to 0,

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:06.130
which means that this term
also has to be greater than

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:07.490
or equal to 0.

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:10.170
But we have just proven that
the local minimum, the gradient

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.810
is equal to 0.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:15.650
So we are left with the
fact that if a point is

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:21.010
a local minimum, then
this quadratic product

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.810
has to be greater
than or equal to 0.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.930
And delta x is arbitrary.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:28.850
It can be, so for
example, if you're into d,

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:33.290
it can be any vector in
any possible direction.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.090
So what is this
telling us with respect

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:38.530
to the Hessian of the function?

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.302
There has to be
positive semi-definite.

00:12:40.302 --> 00:12:42.760
Actually, that's the definition
of a positive semi-definite

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.000
matrix.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.620
So if the function is
twice differentiable,

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:50.080
we get the strengthen
necessary optimality condition

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:53.180
that not only the gradient
has to be equal to 0,

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:58.160
but Hessian has to be
positive semi-definite.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:03.280
Which leads us to
the NOC theorem,

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.440
necessary optimality
condition theorem.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:09.040
Let x star be an unconstrained
local minimum of a function that

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:15.180
maps a vector of n variables
into a scalar, that is,

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:19.120
a real number, R is the
set of real numbers,

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:24.960
Rn is the set of vectors with
components in the real space.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.820
Assume that if f1 is C1.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:32.880
C1 means that the function is
continuously differentiable.

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:36.880
Let's assume that x star is
an unconstrained local minimum

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:45.350
within an open set, then the
gradient has to be equal to 0.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:49.130
And additionally, if f
is twice differentiable,

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:57.990
the Hessian has to be
positive semi definite.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:02.610
A reminder, an open set
is, loosely speaking,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:06.930
a set that contains a
ball around each point.

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:10.430
So basically, a set doesn't
contain its boundary.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:13.670
This is important
because, for example,

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:20.550
say that you have this function
that is defined between a and b.

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:24.590
Is this point here
a local minimum?

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:25.410
Well, it is.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.230
So if you move in any
allowable direction,

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.910
then the value of the
function increases.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:34.590
But is the derivative
equal to 0?

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:37.070
Clearly not because this
will be the derivative.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:40.340
So here what's happening
is at this point

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:45.420
is not inside the open set,
is indeed on the boundary.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:47.020
So this necessary
optimality condition

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.420
only holds if you are
not on the boundary

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.380
of the domain of your function.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:57.660
So that's what the open set
statement here is referring to.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.600
If you are within
the open set, yes,

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:01.300
then if there is
a local minimum,

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:04.500
then the derivative
has to be equal to 0.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:05.380
Yeah.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:08.380
So when you say the open set,
does that mean the same thing

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:10.140
as oversharing?

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:11.380
No.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:16.680
So if, basically, so here.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:26.312
So you can't have an
interval like this one.

00:15:26.312 --> 00:15:28.800
So the interval does
have constraints.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:31.020
So it has a lower bound
as an upper bound,

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:34.660
but does not contain
its boundary.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.040
So it's the notion of open
interval or closed interval.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:43.890
Does that make sense?

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:45.290
Basically, you
don't want to pick

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:49.050
points that are on the
boundary of your domain.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:50.290
That's what he's saying.

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:52.610
We can still have a
constrained problem.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:54.290
If you have constraint,
that's perfect.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.450
Then, of course, the
space of real numbers

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:57.510
doesn't have a boundary.

00:15:57.510 --> 00:15:59.090
So yes, you're in an open set.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:00.270
And so this holds true.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:01.610
No problem.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:04.730
This is more for the
case of a function that,

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:07.850
for example, is defined in--

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:12.150
that maps all numbers from
ab into the real numbers.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:16.050
So the domain is
actually between a and b.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:19.770
You want to make sure that you
apply the NOC condition only

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.690
for points strictly
between a and b.

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:25.010
Otherwise, you might
get a spurious situation

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:27.490
whereby you have a point
that is a local minimum,

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:34.090
but doesn't have a
derivative equal to 0.

00:16:34.090 --> 00:16:35.370
That's all I'm saying.

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:38.390
But the moment you move
away from the boundary,

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.710
then for a point to have any
chance to be a local minimum,

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:45.670
then the derivative
has to be equal to 0.

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:48.670
The more technicality,
by the way.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.350
So then the natural
question is, can we

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:53.850
strengthen this to a sufficient
optimality condition?

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:57.110
So a condition that says
if something happens, then

00:16:57.110 --> 00:17:00.550
that point is a local minimum.

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:03.950
And here I'm just going
to give you the intuition.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:08.550
Say that now we strengthen
the condition whereby

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:13.550
the Hessian now is not just
positive semi-definite,

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.630
but is positive
definite, which means

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:21.109
that as you move
locally around x star,

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:26.510
the value of the
function increases.

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:31.190
So this suggests that
strengthening the condition

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:33.670
has shown to be positive
definite will lead us

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:35.980
to a sufficient
optimality condition.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:37.980
Of course, the proof has
to be a little bit more

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:41.380
involved, because there are some
additional terms that we're not

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:42.640
accounting approximations.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:45.820
So we should use a little
bit of a limit arguments

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:49.120
to make sure that this actually
is mathematically rigorous.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:55.160
But the intuition is that if
Hessian is positive definite,

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.700
the gradient is equal
to 0, the function

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:02.940
locally around x star
is essentially a ball.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:06.780
And so the value of the function
increases immediately after you

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:10.860
leave x star, which means that
the Hessian being positive

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:14.620
definite is indeed a sufficient
condition, which is encapsulated

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:18.262
in the theorem here.

00:18:18.262 --> 00:18:20.220
So for example, one way
to use these conditions

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.860
is that you look at all the
points for which the gradient is

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.125
equal to 0.

00:18:26.125 --> 00:18:27.500
And then maybe
you get six points

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:28.723
that satisfy the condition.

00:18:28.723 --> 00:18:30.140
And then you
evaluate the function

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:31.440
at each one of these points.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.810
And then you play
some other arguments

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:37.970
to see whether that is indeed
a local minimum or not.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:40.090
One special case that
will become important

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:43.930
later is that of
a convex function.

00:18:43.930 --> 00:18:46.550
Probably, you already know
what a convex function is,

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:49.290
but just in case you
don't know about it,

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:54.450
it's a specific class of
function that in a way

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:59.550
have a behavior
that is very nice.

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:03.330
And then I will specify
later what I mean by nice.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.490
But they're still
nonlinear functions.

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:08.530
So, how are convex
functions defined?

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.750
So let's start from the
domain of a convex function.

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:15.330
Convex function is defined
over convex domain.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.490
What is a convex domain?

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:29.850
Well, a set is said to be convex
if whenever you pick two points,

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:33.420
the line joining them
is contained in the set.

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:34.840
So this is a convex set.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:42.040
Mathematically, a subset
of a Euclidean space

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:44.280
is referred to as a
convex set if whenever

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:48.320
you pick two points x and
y, the line joining x and y

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.920
is contained in the set.

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:52.460
So this set is convex.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:59.980
This set is not convex, because
if I pick these two points,

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:03.320
then this line
leaves the set, which

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:05.880
means this set is not convex.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:09.920
So let's consider a
set C which is convex.

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:16.760
A function f is called convex
if this condition holds.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:20.040
What is this condition
say graphically?

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:25.020
So let's just draw a diagram.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:42.350
So you have a function.

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:48.950
So you have x, you
have y, and then

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:52.350
you have a function
such as this one.

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:57.710
So you pick any point
between x and y.

00:20:57.710 --> 00:21:00.390
So for example, this point here.

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:07.050
So this will be alpha
x plus 1 minus alpha y.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:16.830
This will be f of x.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:21.070
This will be f of y.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:28.450
So the function is convex if the
line segments between any two

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:33.910
points on the graph of the
function lies above the graph.

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:46.170
So this will be the segment
that joins fx and fy.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:49.290
The function is
convex if that line

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:52.230
is above the graph
of the function.

00:21:55.050 --> 00:22:05.450
So, for example, this
function is convex and then

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:08.890
this function,
what do you think?

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:09.910
Is not convex.

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:14.370
Because, for example,
if you pick two points,

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:17.690
then the line will
be below the graph.

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:24.525
So you might say, why do you
care about such functions?

00:22:24.525 --> 00:22:26.150
From the point of
view of optimization,

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:27.580
you care for two reasons.

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:33.800
So these functions are special
in that a local minimum of f

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:37.800
over C is also a global minimum.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:40.000
In general, nonlinear
optimization,

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.480
a local minimum
is not guaranteed

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:42.460
to be a global minimum.

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:45.960
You might have many local
minima and then some of them

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:49.160
will be global minima.

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:51.580
In addition, if f
is strictly convex,

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.440
which means that this
inequality holds strictly

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:56.760
for all points not
in the boundary,

00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:02.120
then there is at least at
most one global minimum.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:07.240
Even more interestingly, if f
is convex and is differentiable,

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:09.960
then the gradient
being equal to 0

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:12.760
is a necessary a sufficient
condition for a vector

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:16.200
x star to be a
global minimum of C.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:18.960
So this is nice on two
different accounts.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:23.680
Not only gradient equal to
0 is a sufficient condition

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:25.830
as opposed to just being
a necessary condition

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:28.630
as it is the case for
general nonlinear function,

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:31.810
but also is a
sufficient condition,

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:34.910
not just for local
optimality, but indeed

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:38.030
for global optimality, which
is great because it essentially

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.450
means that a very local test.

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:42.790
So essentially, taking a
gradient means that you're just

00:23:42.790 --> 00:23:44.950
looking at a
function very locally

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:49.070
tells you a property
about the entire function.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:52.270
So you can think how
computationally convex functions

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:54.670
are much easier to deal with.

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:57.190
That's basically the
intuition I want to give you.

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:58.390
Yeah.

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:02.702
You mentioned if the convex
function is differentiable,

00:24:02.702 --> 00:24:06.470
how do convex
functions [INAUDIBLE]?

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.690
For example, if you [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:09.190
Yeah.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:14.250
OK.

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.550
So we'll get back to this later.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:18.110
In particular, one
of the strategies

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:20.710
we look at to compute
the trajectories,

00:24:20.710 --> 00:24:25.420
to solve open loop
optimal control problems

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:32.120
will be to successively
complexify a function,

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:34.980
solve a convex optimization
problem, get a new solution,

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:37.640
reconvexify the
problem, and iterate.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:40.140
It turns out that
this strategy is

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:41.660
one of the most
popular strategies

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:45.660
to do trajectory optimization
for robotic systems

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:46.820
of all sorts.

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:50.642
So we'll come back to
convex optimization later.

00:24:50.642 --> 00:24:52.100
So that's essentially
what I wanted

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:54.400
to say about necessary
optimality conditions.

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:57.440
They are useful to filter
out the candidates.

00:24:57.440 --> 00:25:01.100
They form the basis to derive
a computational algorithm

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:04.740
to compute local minima,
as we will see in a second.

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:07.960
They can also be used for
sensitivity analysis purposes.

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:12.300
So to see how much a
local minimum might change

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:14.900
if we change some
of the problem data,

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.320
like some of the value of the
parameters in your problem,

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:19.260
like the mass of
your robotic system.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.340
Even though the
sensitivity analysis aspect

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:24.730
is a bit beyond the
scope of this class.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:26.090
All right.

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:31.830
So let's then move
to today's lecture.

00:25:36.542 --> 00:25:40.030
So, so far, I've talked about
necessary optimality conditions.

00:25:40.030 --> 00:25:42.770
But oftentimes,
it's quite unwieldy

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:46.050
to compute all the points
that satisfy the condition

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:47.850
gradient equal to 0.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:50.250
So we're interested in
computational methods

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:56.450
that directly give us the
candidate for optimality.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:59.217
So that's what we're
going to see next.

00:25:59.217 --> 00:26:01.050
And then we're going
to conclude the lecture

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:03.490
by expanding what we
know from the field

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:06.730
of unconstrained nonlinear
optimization to the setting

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:09.090
where we have constraints.

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:10.930
Constraints are
indeed something we

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:14.370
have to deal with in this
class, because at the minimum,

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:17.170
we have a one constraint
we have to deal with,

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:19.550
which is the basic constraints
we have to deal with.

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:28.270
The demise of our body.

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:30.530
I mean, of course, we cannot
go at the speed of light,

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:32.570
so that's a constraint
on our motion.

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:35.230
So we can optimize
as much as we want.

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:38.350
But if you have a Fiat
500, you don't get

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:40.070
the performance of a Ferrari.

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:43.250
So that's probably
unfortunate fact of life.

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:44.210
So yeah.

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:48.510
So at least, there is
dynamics as a constraint.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:54.050
So let's talk a little bit
about computational methods.

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:55.590
Of course, this
class will be quite

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:59.002
deep in computational methods to
solve optimal control problems.

00:26:59.002 --> 00:27:00.710
And what we're going
to discuss now again

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:03.030
is quite pedagogical with
respect to what we're

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:05.350
going to discuss next.

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:07.990
And particularly, I mean,
there are many different ways

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:12.050
to attempt to solve a
nonlinear optimization problem,

00:27:12.050 --> 00:27:14.590
but the specific
way we look at is

00:27:14.590 --> 00:27:19.740
referred to as
gradient methods, which

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:23.040
is a particular class
of iterative methods.

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:25.180
So what are iterative methods.

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:27.700
So the idea is that
we want to solve

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:31.900
our unconstrained non-linear
optimization problem.

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:42.660
And we want to see whether we
can find some rule that starting

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:46.340
from, to use this
superscript, yes,

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:50.380
starting from some initial guess
of what a local minimum could

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:51.740
be.

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:53.840
If you have no idea,
you can just say, well,

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:58.780
your local guess is 0,
0, 0,0, just the origin.

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:07.100
Then you start producing a
sequence of updated guesses.

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:10.140
And the idea is that
you hope that you

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:14.700
have a rule that allows you
to update your guesses in such

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:21.450
a way that f of x0 is
greater than of f of x1

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:26.410
is greater than or equal
f of x2 dot, dot, dot.

00:28:26.410 --> 00:28:30.250
So basically, you try
to iteratively update

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:35.770
your guess about what a local
minimum can be with a property

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:39.330
that at every new guess,
the value of the function

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:43.170
is below the value of the
functions of the previous guess,

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:47.450
with the hope that after
sufficiently many updates,

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:51.330
the value of the function has
been reduced so much that you

00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:53.770
have other rich local
minimum or at least you're

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:55.190
very close to local minimum.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:59.410
But the question, of
course, is how do you

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:00.930
produce such a rule
that allows you

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:03.390
to go through these iterates?

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:09.650
And, in doing so, you decrease
the value of the function.

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:12.410
So for that, we
again use the notion

00:29:12.410 --> 00:29:16.320
of a Taylor series approximation
of first-order approximation

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:17.840
of a function.

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:37.200
Specifically, let's assume
that our current guess

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:44.490
is x some vector with components
taking value 1, 3, minus 2,

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:47.360
or 1, an assignment.

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:52.840
So let's consider the
updated guess obtained

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:59.160
by taking x and
subtracting alpha times

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:02.280
gradient of f evaluated at x.

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:07.480
I call this a new
guess as x alpha.

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:09.220
It is parameterized by alpha.

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:10.900
Alpha is a positive number.

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:16.040
So basically, I am at x.

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:20.000
Assume that you
are in the plane.

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:32.280
You are in the plane.

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.620
So this is x.

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:39.240
This is gradient of f.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:41.040
Remember that the
gradient is a vector.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:47.660
So x plus gradient
of f leads you here.

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:50.920
But of course, this is
parameterized by alpha.

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:56.260
So the larger the alpha, the
more far away you move from x.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:31:04.280
So then, over here.

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:05.280
OK.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:08.100
So I call it x alpha.

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:15.730
So then we know that f of x of
alpha if alpha is small enough,

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:18.490
so I don't move too
far away from x,

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:21.050
is equal to f of x, or
at least approximately

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:37.970
equal to f of x plus gradient
of f of x times x alpha minus x.

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:48.690
But x alpha minus x is equal to
minus alpha gradient of f of x.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:32:01.450
So what we got is that this
is equal to or equal to f of x

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:05.450
minus alpha gradient of f of x.

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:12.400
Here there is a prime,
prime gradient of f of x.

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:21.000
But you know that for any vector
v in Rn, v scalar product with v

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:27.120
is equal to the
square norm of v.

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:34.160
So this is equal to f of x
minus alpha gradient of f

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:36.660
of x squared.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:45.760
The norm is a
non-negative number.

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:48.860
In particular, if the
gradient is equal to 0,

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:50.400
well, you already
know that you are

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:51.860
a candidate to local minimum.

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:55.400
But if it is not equal
to 0, then this number

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:58.960
is positive, which means
that this number is negative,

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:06.560
which means that f of x alpha
has a lower value than f of x.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:09.080
So I've actually
found a direction

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:13.590
that leads me to a value for
f that is lower than the value

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:17.510
that I have at
the current guess.

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:21.530
Now, interestingly, as alpha
gets larger and larger,

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:23.550
which means that I move
farther and farther

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:27.170
away from x, this becomes
larger and larger,

00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:30.310
which means that f of x alpha
becomes smaller and smaller.

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:32.830
So you might be tempted to
say, so why don't you shoot

00:33:32.830 --> 00:33:36.470
alpha to a very large number?

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:37.650
Why you don't do that?

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:39.110
I mean, it's tempting, right?

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:41.910
Why don't we choose
alpha equal a million?

00:33:41.910 --> 00:33:43.470
Skip over the minimum.

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:44.630
What's that?

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:47.253
Skip over the minimum.

00:33:47.253 --> 00:33:48.670
Well, from this
mathematics, there

00:33:48.670 --> 00:33:50.070
is nothing that is
telling us that it's

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:51.112
keeping over the minimum.

00:33:51.112 --> 00:33:53.070
We just cannot arbitrarily
decrease the value

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:54.250
of the function.

00:33:54.250 --> 00:33:56.350
[? For the ?]
approximation [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:56.350 --> 00:34:00.190
Exactly, which actually is
related to what you were saying,

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:02.690
that when we do
the approximation,

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:04.270
the first-order
approximation, always

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.790
keep in mind that
this holds true

00:34:06.790 --> 00:34:11.739
for points that are
very close to x.

00:34:11.739 --> 00:34:13.800
So the moment that
you move farther away,

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:16.722
you might think that you're
doing something useful,

00:34:16.722 --> 00:34:18.139
but in reality,
you're not relying

00:34:18.139 --> 00:34:20.900
on an approximation
that is telling you

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:22.600
nothing about the
value of the function.

00:34:22.600 --> 00:34:27.340
So the result will no
longer be applicable.

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:28.699
Make sense?

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:30.900
Effectively, it means
that you go, maybe

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:34.460
overshoot a local minimum.

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:39.020
But mathematically,
one might say, well,

00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:41.100
if the approximation
was true, actually, you

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:45.540
could just arbitrarily decrease
the value of the function.

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:51.540
So this leads us to the
class of gradient methods.

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:55.260
The class of gradient
methods is a bit broader

00:34:55.260 --> 00:35:00.780
in that you can slightly
generalize this construction.

00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:10.840
But just saying, by looking at
update rules, whereby x of alpha

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:20.480
is more generally defined
as x plus alpha d, where

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:28.260
the gradient of f at x scalar
product with the direction d,

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:31.520
so d is a vector,
is greater than 0.

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:33.440
Sorry is less than 0.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:40.240
Of course, if d is equal to the
minus of the gradient, which

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:44.500
is exactly what we have here,
automatically this is verified.

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:48.560
But my point here is that you
don't need to necessarily go

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:50.300
along the gradient direction.

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:53.160
You can also pick some
other vector, as long

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:56.520
as this property is satisfied,
because if you do so

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:58.620
and you go through
the same steps, again,

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:02.680
you will find out
that f of x alpha

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:05.670
has lower value than f
of x because this scalar

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:07.910
product, the
direction d is chosen,

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:10.830
such that this scalar
product is negative.

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:13.568
So geometrically, what
is happening here?

00:36:13.568 --> 00:36:15.110
It's always good to
have a little bit

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:16.610
of a geometric intuition.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:24.130
So say that you have a function
defined on the real plane.

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:26.790
So on the plane of the board.

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:32.290
And let's consider the
so-called level set curves.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:35.830
So basically, the set of
points x, such as f of x

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:38.143
is equal to a given number c.

00:36:38.143 --> 00:36:40.310
And then you can change the
number, c is equal to 1,

00:36:40.310 --> 00:36:41.710
2, and so on.

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:52.450
So this could be, for
example, the level set curves.

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:57.150
So this would be
f of x equal c3.

00:36:57.150 --> 00:37:05.260
This one, f of x equals c2, f
of x-- so for this one, f of x

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:08.780
equals c1 for this one.

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:11.180
And assume that
the C3 is greater

00:37:11.180 --> 00:37:16.100
than c2 is greater
than c1, and so on.

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:18.880
So say that we are here at x.

00:37:21.900 --> 00:37:25.660
As you should know, otherwise,
you should look at a proof,

00:37:25.660 --> 00:37:33.140
the gradient of a function
is orthogonal to the level

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:34.220
set curves.

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:35.740
So basically, what
this update rule

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.900
is telling me is
that I'm looking

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:42.420
at moving along these directions
here, parameterized by alpha.

00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:48.340
Or I can look at some other
directions, like these ones,

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:52.600
as long as the scalar
product is negative,

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:55.440
which means that I form an
angle greater than 90 degrees.

00:38:02.650 --> 00:38:04.890
So this leads us to
the celebrated class

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:07.890
of gradient methods,
iterative rules whereby

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:13.250
your next guess, so your guess
of what the local minimum is

00:38:13.250 --> 00:38:16.850
at iteration k plus 1 is
equal to your previous guess

00:38:16.850 --> 00:38:20.730
after k iterations
plus alpha k, dk.

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:24.210
Alpha k here doesn't mean
alpha to the power of k.

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:26.350
Here it just means
that the step size,

00:38:26.350 --> 00:38:29.970
as we will see in a second,
might change in value depending

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:34.890
on which iteration you are at.

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:37.970
So if the gradient
is different from 0

00:38:37.970 --> 00:38:41.490
and dk is chosen such
that the angle between dk

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:46.250
and the gradient is
greater than 90 degrees

00:38:46.250 --> 00:38:49.170
and the step size alpha
is chosen to be positive,

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:53.210
this is guaranteed to be--

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:56.370
alpha chosen is carefully as
we'll discuss in a second.

00:38:56.370 --> 00:39:01.320
This will lead to descent.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:03.000
Does that make sense?

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:05.760
So gradient methods,
again, iterative algorithms

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:10.360
where you update your guess
according to this rule, where

00:39:10.360 --> 00:39:14.520
the dk is a vector
with a property that

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:17.520
is scalar product with the
gradient is less than 0.

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:20.460
And the step size alpha
is chosen to be positive.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:26.160
And typically, we want
to choose alpha, such

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:30.800
that the value at
the new guess is

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:33.280
below the value at
the previous guess.

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:35.520
Practically, you
want to be careful

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:37.840
that you don't
choose alpha too big.

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:40.560
Does the guarantee still
hold if you take into account

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:43.840
the second-order term?

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:44.680
Yes.

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:49.272
This will lead us to, so you
can reason also the level

00:39:49.272 --> 00:39:50.480
of-- so there are two things.

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:55.600
You can look-- you can still use
information from the Hessian,

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:58.120
as you will see in
a second, or you

00:39:58.120 --> 00:40:00.300
can find more
sophisticated algorithms

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:03.703
that consider more
sophisticated approximations.

00:40:03.703 --> 00:40:05.120
We're going to
look at the former.

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:07.900
So I'll show you in one
second why actually we

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:10.240
want to use information
also from the Hessian,

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:11.560
not just from the gradient.

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:19.740
So, of course, there are two
things that are unresolved yet.

00:40:19.740 --> 00:40:21.560
So how do we pick the
descent direction?

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:23.560
We said that the
gradient is a choice.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:27.900
But really any vector
d, as long as the scalar

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:30.680
product with the gradient is
less than 0 is a valid choice.

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:32.300
So which one do you pick?

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:34.200
And how do you pick alpha?

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:36.500
So alpha is this
number here, which is

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:40.020
referred to as the step size.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:45.300
So for the descent
direction, a popular class

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:50.300
of descent directions is defined
as the gradient pre-multiplied

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:54.260
by a matrix that is
positive definite.

00:40:54.260 --> 00:41:01.790
Of course, with this choice
of the descent direction,

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:05.530
the scalar product between
the gradient of f and d

00:41:05.530 --> 00:41:09.290
is negative because we've
chosen d to be positive definite

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:11.530
and we have a minus in front.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:16.170
So this is a valid assignment
of the descent direction

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:18.530
according to our rules.

00:41:18.530 --> 00:41:19.990
But we just shifted the problem.

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:21.910
So how do we pick the direction?

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:26.770
Now the question is, how
do we pick this matrix d?

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:31.710
Two popular choices are you
pick d simply as the identity.

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:34.010
So basically, you just move
along the negative gradient

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:37.610
direction, or you
pick d, as you were

00:41:37.610 --> 00:41:41.610
discussing with your colleague,
as the inverse of the Hessian.

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:43.810
Let me provide some
intuition about why

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:48.270
we may want to consider
different choices of d

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:52.410
without going too much into
the details about how one would

00:41:52.410 --> 00:41:54.270
derive actually these rules.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:04.600
Specifically, let's assume that
we have a function still defined

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:11.580
in 2D with a very
nice level set curves.

00:42:11.580 --> 00:42:14.040
So they are just circles.

00:42:14.040 --> 00:42:18.280
So this is the domain of
the function x1 and x2.

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:21.120
So the function
would have values

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:23.820
that go orthogonal
to the blackboard.

00:42:23.820 --> 00:42:28.320
And it will be a
ball, isotropic ball.

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:30.440
So say that you're here.

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:32.320
So the gradient shoots
in this direction

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:34.880
is orthogonal to
the level set car.

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:36.800
Clearly, this is the
minimum because we're

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:37.820
talking about a ball.

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:39.840
So the ball is really
at-- so basically,

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:44.080
the function would be x1
squared plus x2 squared.

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:46.520
The minimum will
be at the origin.

00:42:46.520 --> 00:42:51.600
So you move toward the negative
direction of the gradient.

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:55.040
It's clear that you're pointing
that in the right direction.

00:42:55.040 --> 00:42:56.410
You pick some alpha.

00:42:56.410 --> 00:42:58.190
Maybe you end up here.

00:42:58.190 --> 00:42:59.650
Then you recompute the gradient.

00:42:59.650 --> 00:43:02.030
Maybe you end up here, and
then finally, you end up here.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:04.350
Great.

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:13.330
But what if we are in a
situation like the following,

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:17.370
whereby our function is still
defined on two dimensions,

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:21.410
but now the level set
curves are ellipses?

00:43:24.470 --> 00:43:28.470
So it's still a ball, but now
it's elongated in one direction

00:43:28.470 --> 00:43:31.550
and shrunk in the
other direction.

00:43:31.550 --> 00:43:34.390
So say that you are here.

00:43:34.390 --> 00:43:37.530
Now the gradient points
in this direction

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:40.670
and the negative gradient
points in this direction.

00:43:40.670 --> 00:43:44.950
So, as you see, actually,
you're not pointing directly

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:45.850
to the minimum.

00:43:45.850 --> 00:43:46.810
The minimum is still 0.

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:47.570
It's still a ball.

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:50.510
It's just this is
elongating this direction

00:43:50.510 --> 00:43:52.970
and shrunk in the
lateral direction.

00:43:55.580 --> 00:44:00.180
So this is to say that
peaking as a descent

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:03.842
direction, the negative
gradient may not necessarily

00:44:03.842 --> 00:44:05.300
be the best thing
to do, especially

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:09.020
if you have a level set curves
that are very elongated,

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:15.860
what happens is that you
will start zigzagging a lot.

00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:17.460
So that, and here
I'm just giving you

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:20.100
the motivation that there could
be a little bit of derivation

00:44:20.100 --> 00:44:21.140
to do it.

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:24.540
That's why you may want
to use the Hessian.

00:44:24.540 --> 00:44:26.740
Intuitively, the
Hessian gives you

00:44:26.740 --> 00:44:30.080
information about the
curvature of your function.

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:33.100
So it allows you
to better inform

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:37.020
the direction that looks
most promising in terms

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:38.820
of going to a minimum.

00:44:38.820 --> 00:44:42.420
So it basically allows you to
recognize that this problem is

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:43.638
different from this problem.

00:44:43.638 --> 00:44:45.180
So you should do
something different.

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:49.320
That's roughly speaking what
these action here I'm doing.

00:44:49.320 --> 00:44:51.420
I won't go further
into the details

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:54.200
since this is not a class
on nonlinear optimization.

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:58.200
So just to speak, there
are different choices.

00:44:58.200 --> 00:44:59.640
This seems better.

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:01.140
Of course, it uses
more information.

00:45:01.140 --> 00:45:03.260
It uses information about the
curvature of the function.

00:45:03.260 --> 00:45:04.740
But everything comes at a cost.

00:45:04.740 --> 00:45:07.440
Here the cost is that if you
want to implement this descent

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:10.395
direction, not only you
have to compute a gradient,

00:45:10.395 --> 00:45:12.020
but you also have to
compute a Hessian.

00:45:12.020 --> 00:45:14.720
So basically, a matrix of
second-order derivatives.

00:45:14.720 --> 00:45:18.360
Depending on the size of your
nonlinear optimization problem,

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:20.300
maybe there is not
enough bang for the buck.

00:45:20.300 --> 00:45:23.280
Sure, you might decrease
the number of iterations

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:26.500
because your direction is better
aligned with the local minima

00:45:26.500 --> 00:45:27.680
eventually will be.

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:30.040
But every iteration is
much more expensive.

00:45:30.040 --> 00:45:31.840
So this is just to
say that, and we'll

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:34.640
see this theme over
and over, that there

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:36.400
is a little bit of
a trade-off in terms

00:45:36.400 --> 00:45:38.280
of how much
sophistication you want

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:41.920
to have in your algorithm
versus the algorithm

00:45:41.920 --> 00:45:43.383
that eventually
you end up using,

00:45:43.383 --> 00:45:45.800
since eventually, your goal
is to get to the local minimum

00:45:45.800 --> 00:45:48.618
as fast as possible.

00:45:48.618 --> 00:45:50.660
So we talked a little bit
about this interaction.

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:54.030
What about alpha, the step size?

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:57.010
Again, different ways of
choosing the step size.

00:45:57.010 --> 00:46:01.310
For example, a possible way is
the so-called minimization rule.

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:05.750
Among all the
positive alphas, you

00:46:05.750 --> 00:46:09.310
choose the one that leads
you to the largest cost

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:12.070
reduction into your function f.

00:46:12.070 --> 00:46:16.990
Sensible, but you have to
solve the minimization problem.

00:46:16.990 --> 00:46:20.230
Not terrible minimizing
over a scalar,

00:46:20.230 --> 00:46:24.430
but still it's minimization
problem that you want to solve.

00:46:24.430 --> 00:46:27.010
At the end of the spectrum,
you're super lazy.

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:30.740
So you just pick a number
for alpha, say 0.1.

00:46:30.740 --> 00:46:31.490
You stick with it.

00:46:31.490 --> 00:46:33.950
It's probably the very
first descent algorithm

00:46:33.950 --> 00:46:35.190
that you would implement.

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:38.270
You just pick a value for
alpha, you stick with it

00:46:38.270 --> 00:46:39.910
and you hope for the best.

00:46:39.910 --> 00:46:43.752
Unfortunately, though,
with this choice,

00:46:43.752 --> 00:46:45.210
actually, the method
might diverge,

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:47.870
so it might not converge
to a local minimum,

00:46:47.870 --> 00:46:51.020
and a convergence rate
could be very slow.

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:53.060
So another idea that
is quite popular

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:55.740
is the so-called
diminishing stepsize.

00:46:55.740 --> 00:47:00.560
So you choose an alpha that
across iterations goes to 0.

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:06.120
So you start with 1,
maybe 0.5, 0.1, 0.005.

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:08.460
So basically, you
have a rule whereby

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:12.340
alpha goes to 0,
but with a property

00:47:12.340 --> 00:47:15.260
that the sum over all
alphas across iteration

00:47:15.260 --> 00:47:17.980
is equal to infinity.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:20.060
So what's the idea here?

00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:23.220
The idea here is
that oftentimes,

00:47:23.220 --> 00:47:27.780
when you start the
process, you are--

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:30.320
so let's look at now a
one-dimensional problem.

00:47:35.260 --> 00:47:38.940
So you're here.

00:47:38.940 --> 00:47:41.860
So you are quite far
from the local minimum.

00:47:41.860 --> 00:47:43.980
So at the beginning,
it's quite typical

00:47:43.980 --> 00:47:46.420
to have a long stepsizes.

00:47:46.420 --> 00:47:49.250
So you make a big jump.

00:47:49.250 --> 00:47:51.730
And maybe you move here,
then you move here.

00:47:51.730 --> 00:47:54.490
Maybe then you overshoot.

00:47:54.490 --> 00:47:56.930
But then after a while,
where conceivably you

00:47:56.930 --> 00:47:59.150
have gotten closer
to local minimum,

00:47:59.150 --> 00:48:02.810
you start making smaller
and smaller jumps,

00:48:02.810 --> 00:48:07.390
so that you don't oscillate too
much around the local minimum.

00:48:07.390 --> 00:48:11.490
And eventually,
you converge to it.

00:48:11.490 --> 00:48:13.370
So at the beginning,
very large alpha, so

00:48:13.370 --> 00:48:15.195
that you can make big progress.

00:48:15.195 --> 00:48:16.570
But the point with
large alpha is

00:48:16.570 --> 00:48:18.510
that as you get close
to the local minimum,

00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:20.910
you can start bouncing
around and oscillating.

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:23.170
But then you start
decreasing the alpha

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:25.850
so that you do that
final fine tuning

00:48:25.850 --> 00:48:29.802
to really get to the local
minima you care about.

00:48:29.802 --> 00:48:32.010
But you have to be careful
because if your alpha goes

00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:36.410
to zero too quickly, maybe
you don't make enough steps

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:37.970
toward your local minimum.

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:41.490
So you want to make
sure that collectively,

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:43.320
when you sum all of
the alpha, actually,

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:46.520
you may make potentially
an infinite distance,

00:48:46.520 --> 00:48:51.400
so that you are guaranteed to
explore widely if necessary.

00:48:51.400 --> 00:48:54.300
[INAUDIBLE]

00:48:56.840 --> 00:48:57.860
In the first iteration?

00:48:57.860 --> 00:48:58.360
Yes.

00:48:58.360 --> 00:49:00.400
Generally pure guess.

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:02.740
Yeah.

00:49:02.740 --> 00:49:08.800
There is quite a bit of
hyperparameter tuning,

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:11.180
which is a good segue
into this analysis.

00:49:11.180 --> 00:49:14.560
There is a lot of stuff
that we haven't discussed.

00:49:14.560 --> 00:49:16.780
How do you choose
those parameters?

00:49:16.780 --> 00:49:20.040
So for the descent direction,
it is a bit more principled.

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:24.680
For stepsize, it is a bit murky,
it's a bit more experienced.

00:49:24.680 --> 00:49:26.920
But in general, there
are three key concepts

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:28.640
we haven't really discussed.

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:32.880
The first one is, how do we
guarantee that this algorithm

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:33.980
indeed converges?

00:49:33.980 --> 00:49:37.080
So it told you how,
in one step, you're

00:49:37.080 --> 00:49:39.180
guaranteed to decrease
the value of the function.

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:40.957
But that doesn't
mean necessarily

00:49:40.957 --> 00:49:43.040
that you're guaranteed to
get to the local minimum

00:49:43.040 --> 00:49:46.980
once you iterate across
all of the iterations.

00:49:46.980 --> 00:49:48.500
So indeed, there
are some properties

00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:52.620
that one could list that
ensure that you converge

00:49:52.620 --> 00:49:54.380
to a local minimum, that is--

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:55.800
sorry, to stationary points.

00:49:55.800 --> 00:50:00.300
That means a point where
the gradient is equal to 0.

00:50:00.300 --> 00:50:01.880
Second, when do you stop?

00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:06.440
There are different
termination rules.

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:09.880
One might just say I'm willing
to invest 100 iterations.

00:50:09.880 --> 00:50:12.740
Whatever I get after 100
iterations, that's it.

00:50:12.740 --> 00:50:21.780
Or maybe you look at the value
of the function at xk plus 1

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:25.260
and the value of
the function at xk.

00:50:25.260 --> 00:50:26.760
You take the difference.

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:29.420
If this difference
is less than epsilon,

00:50:29.420 --> 00:50:32.220
you just quit and call it a
day, means that you're not

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:34.540
doing much progress anymore.

00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:38.700
And so there is no point in
keeping investing computation.

00:50:38.700 --> 00:50:40.080
And then convergence rate.

00:50:40.080 --> 00:50:42.180
So depending on the
initial condition

00:50:42.180 --> 00:50:45.450
and how actually you choose
the stepsize profile,

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:47.410
what can you say about
how quickly it will

00:50:47.410 --> 00:50:50.490
converge to a stationary point?

00:50:50.490 --> 00:50:53.023
We could spend pretty much half
of the class on this topic,

00:50:53.023 --> 00:50:54.190
so we are skipping on those.

00:50:54.190 --> 00:50:56.410
But I just want you
to be aware of those.

00:50:56.410 --> 00:51:00.330
And second of all, the class of
methods that I just discussed

00:51:00.330 --> 00:51:03.410
are referred to as
gradient methods

00:51:03.410 --> 00:51:05.570
because you use
information from gradient.

00:51:05.570 --> 00:51:10.570
But there are also methods that
do not need or necessarily need

00:51:10.570 --> 00:51:13.170
information from the gradient.

00:51:13.170 --> 00:51:16.470
Those are referred to as
derivative free methods,

00:51:16.470 --> 00:51:22.490
such as the coordinate descent
method that iteratively

00:51:22.490 --> 00:51:26.290
minimizes along the
coordinate directions

00:51:26.290 --> 00:51:28.530
to find the minimum
of a function

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:32.210
or the Nelder-Mead method that
is another clever method that

00:51:32.210 --> 00:51:35.730
doesn't require at all any
computation of derivatives.

00:51:35.730 --> 00:51:41.250
This is just to say that there
is a very rich literature,

00:51:41.250 --> 00:51:44.600
both in the field of
gradient-free and non-gradient

00:51:44.600 --> 00:51:47.120
free methods.

00:51:47.120 --> 00:51:48.060
Any questions?

00:51:53.652 --> 00:51:56.880
So, so far we've talked about
unconstrained non-linear

00:51:56.880 --> 00:51:58.640
optimization problems.

00:51:58.640 --> 00:52:00.400
But we are
particularly interested

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:03.580
in constrained optimization
problems because, as I said,

00:52:03.580 --> 00:52:05.160
optimal control
problems typically

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:07.320
have at least one
constraint, which

00:52:07.320 --> 00:52:12.200
is represented by the dynamics.

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:15.080
And we will see the
key solution to solve

00:52:15.080 --> 00:52:17.280
the constrained optimization
problems boils down

00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:20.000
to the concept of a
Lagrange multiplier.

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:23.240
And actually, this concept
will carry over also

00:52:23.240 --> 00:52:26.920
to the setting of
optimal control.

00:52:26.920 --> 00:52:30.560
And we won't do proofs
here, but just wanted

00:52:30.560 --> 00:52:33.520
to give some intuition
about how people would

00:52:33.520 --> 00:52:37.400
go about proving statements
about equality constraints

00:52:37.400 --> 00:52:40.190
problem or inequality
constraint problems.

00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:42.510
Typically, there are
two main approaches.

00:52:42.510 --> 00:52:47.030
The first one is the
so-called penalty viewpoint,

00:52:47.030 --> 00:52:50.310
whereby you take
the constraints,

00:52:50.310 --> 00:52:52.750
you put those
constraints if you,

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:56.430
so to speak, in a cost function
and you assign a penalty

00:52:56.430 --> 00:52:57.950
for violating them.

00:52:57.950 --> 00:53:00.310
So basically, you reduce
a constraint problem

00:53:00.310 --> 00:53:03.950
into an unconstrained one
where constraint satisfaction

00:53:03.950 --> 00:53:06.030
is viewed as a
penalty cost, meaning

00:53:06.030 --> 00:53:08.570
that if you satisfy
the constraint,

00:53:08.570 --> 00:53:10.290
then you have zero in the cost.

00:53:10.290 --> 00:53:12.170
If you don't satisfy
the constraint,

00:53:12.170 --> 00:53:14.630
then the cost shoots up.

00:53:14.630 --> 00:53:15.710
That's one way.

00:53:15.710 --> 00:53:20.110
Or the other way is to take an
approach similar to the one we

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:23.430
have discussed in the case of
unconstrained optimization,

00:53:23.430 --> 00:53:26.990
but when we talk
about displacement,

00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:29.710
we can't talk about
arbitrary displacement

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:31.670
anymore because
some directions are

00:53:31.670 --> 00:53:33.550
allowed, some others
are not, because we

00:53:33.550 --> 00:53:35.090
have equality constraints.

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:38.150
So we have to have a little more
sophisticated notion of what

00:53:38.150 --> 00:53:40.010
an allowable displacement is.

00:53:40.010 --> 00:53:44.730
And based on that, derive
necessary optimality conditions.

00:53:44.730 --> 00:53:47.350
So let's start first with
the equality constraints.

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:50.490
So equality constraint
optimization problems

00:53:50.490 --> 00:53:54.890
are those whereby you're still
trying to minimize a function f

00:53:54.890 --> 00:53:56.110
over real n.

00:53:56.110 --> 00:54:00.370
So over n decision
variables subject to m

00:54:00.370 --> 00:54:04.830
equality constraints
written as hi(x) equals 0.

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:10.250
And we have m of
these functions.

00:54:10.250 --> 00:54:16.170
As I said, f maps Rn into R
and hi also map Rn into R.

00:54:16.170 --> 00:54:19.050
And here we assume that
they are both continuously

00:54:19.050 --> 00:54:21.730
differentiable.

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:24.110
And just to save
on some notation,

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:29.270
I will compactly write the set
of functions h1 to hn as bold h.

00:54:29.270 --> 00:54:32.230
In general, in this class,
a boldface means vector.

00:54:35.410 --> 00:54:41.040
So the basic result in the field
of equality constraint problems

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:45.920
is the Lagrange
multiplier theorem.

00:54:45.920 --> 00:54:49.240
That is a bit cryptic, and I
will provide some intuition

00:54:49.240 --> 00:54:50.200
later on.

00:54:50.200 --> 00:54:55.080
But mechanically , it states
that if x star is a local

00:54:55.080 --> 00:55:00.080
minimum, then there
exists m numbers.

00:55:00.080 --> 00:55:02.920
M is the same number of
equality constraints.

00:55:02.920 --> 00:55:06.080
These numbers are referred to
as the Lagrange multipliers

00:55:06.080 --> 00:55:08.880
and denoted as lambda
1, lambda 2, all the way

00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:12.720
to lambda m such that
the gradient of f

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:18.920
plus the linear combination
of the constraint gradients

00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:23.920
scaled by the Lagrange
multipliers, one per constraint

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:24.940
is equal to 0.

00:55:29.080 --> 00:55:31.120
I'll provide later a
bit of justification.

00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:36.510
Right now, this seems
a bit mysterious.

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:41.670
But first, let me show you
the mechanics about how

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:45.110
people use this theorem.

00:55:45.110 --> 00:55:47.770
So before, in
unconstrained optimization,

00:55:47.770 --> 00:55:50.930
we take the function, we
compute the gradient equal to 0.

00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:53.310
You find the point that
satisfies the condition.

00:55:53.310 --> 00:55:55.510
And then among those
points, you see which

00:55:55.510 --> 00:55:57.630
one achieves the lowest value.

00:55:57.630 --> 00:55:58.910
Great.

00:55:58.910 --> 00:56:03.550
Here we do something
similar, but we don't just

00:56:03.550 --> 00:56:05.170
set the gradient equal to 0.

00:56:05.170 --> 00:56:06.210
That will be the case.

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:10.790
Look how if the number of
constraint is equal to 0,

00:56:10.790 --> 00:56:12.610
this piece goes away.

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:15.190
So we recover the condition
greater than or equal to 0.

00:56:15.190 --> 00:56:17.070
Here is a refinement.

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:18.810
It's not just the
gradient equal to 0,

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:22.430
it's gradient plus the linear
combination of the constraint

00:56:22.430 --> 00:56:25.190
gradient being equal to 0.

00:56:25.190 --> 00:56:26.730
What does that mean in practice?

00:56:26.730 --> 00:56:31.110
So let's assume that we have
this optimization problem, which

00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:32.650
is equality constraint.

00:56:32.650 --> 00:56:35.260
This is the objective function.

00:56:35.260 --> 00:56:38.300
And this is the constraint set.

00:56:38.300 --> 00:56:41.720
So essentially, the function
that we are looking at--

00:56:47.940 --> 00:56:51.680
so this is the plane x1, x2.

00:56:54.500 --> 00:56:59.220
So now the function is not
mapping any possible point

00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:03.860
into a value, but
only those points that

00:57:03.860 --> 00:57:07.680
belong to the circle.

00:57:07.680 --> 00:57:11.380
So this is the locus of
points whereby x1 squared

00:57:11.380 --> 00:57:14.580
plus x2 squared equal to.

00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:17.980
So this is basically
the feasible domain.

00:57:17.980 --> 00:57:21.820
You can pick any point in here.

00:57:21.820 --> 00:57:27.820
And you want to find the one
for which f, namely x1 plus x2

00:57:27.820 --> 00:57:29.280
achieves lowest value.

00:57:33.690 --> 00:57:37.650
So mechanically, what do you do?

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:39.470
This is a necessary
optimality condition.

00:57:39.470 --> 00:57:43.170
So you treat it the same way
as the gradient equal to 0.

00:57:43.170 --> 00:57:47.010
But now there is
that additional term

00:57:47.010 --> 00:57:48.850
that you have to account for.

00:57:48.850 --> 00:57:51.570
And note that you also have
to solve for the Lagrange

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:53.810
multipliers.

00:57:53.810 --> 00:57:58.450
So what you do is
first, you compute

00:57:58.450 --> 00:58:03.710
the gradient of f at here.

00:58:03.710 --> 00:58:09.730
So what the gradient of f is
for a generic point x1, x2?

00:58:09.730 --> 00:58:11.490
Should ask the CA.

00:58:11.490 --> 00:58:12.490
And joke.

00:58:12.490 --> 00:58:21.890
So it's 1, 1 because remember
that the gradient is,

00:58:21.890 --> 00:58:24.810
by definition gradient
of f with respect to x1,

00:58:24.810 --> 00:58:28.170
gradient of f with
respect to x2,

00:58:28.170 --> 00:58:30.630
but f is equal to x1 plus x2.

00:58:30.630 --> 00:58:34.830
So derivative of x1 plus x2 with
respect to x1 is equal to 1.

00:58:34.830 --> 00:58:40.020
Derivative of x1 plus x2 with
respect to x2 is equal to 1.

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:42.650
So then what is
the gradient of h?

00:58:45.310 --> 00:58:53.590
So derivative of x1 squared plus
x2 squared minus 2 with respect

00:58:53.590 --> 00:58:55.990
to x1 is equal to 2x1.

00:59:00.550 --> 00:59:04.990
And for the other
component, it's just 2x2.

00:59:04.990 --> 00:59:10.970
So then what you do is that
you put everything together.

00:59:14.510 --> 00:59:16.530
And so the Lagrange
multiplier theorem,

00:59:16.530 --> 00:59:26.030
it says that if x is a minimum,
then 1, 1 plus lambda, here

00:59:26.030 --> 00:59:27.453
we only have one constraint.

00:59:27.453 --> 00:59:29.870
So we're going to have only
one Lagrange multiplier, which

00:59:29.870 --> 00:59:31.100
I call lambda.

00:59:31.100 --> 00:59:38.720
Lambda 2x1, 2x2 is equal to 0.

00:59:41.420 --> 00:59:45.420
And I have to solve
for x1, x2, and lambda.

00:59:45.420 --> 00:59:48.460
Once I solve for
x1, x2, and lambda,

00:59:48.460 --> 00:59:49.960
I don't care about
lambda anymore.

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:53.380
That was just a mechanism for me
to find the necessary optimality

00:59:53.380 --> 00:59:54.680
conditions for x1, x2.

00:59:54.680 --> 00:59:58.500
I only look at x1,
x2 that had derived.

00:59:58.500 --> 01:00:03.660
But two equations,
three unknowns.

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:05.100
That's a problem.

01:00:05.100 --> 01:00:09.840
Do you have an idea where the
last equation can come from?

01:00:13.000 --> 01:00:13.500
Exactly.

01:00:13.500 --> 01:00:16.380
So still the necessary
[? mathematic ?] condition

01:00:16.380 --> 01:00:19.280
says that x has to
satisfy this condition.

01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:21.040
And of course, x2
has to be feasible.

01:00:21.040 --> 01:00:23.700
So it has to belong
to this circle.

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:26.300
So we also have
additional equations

01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:31.810
x1 squared plus x2
squared equal 2.

01:00:31.810 --> 01:00:35.230
So now we just have
to solve all of this.

01:00:40.250 --> 01:00:47.190
So we have 1 plus 2
lambda x1 equals 0,

01:00:47.190 --> 01:00:52.370
a plus 2 lambda
x2 equals 0, which

01:00:52.370 --> 01:01:01.730
means x1 is equal to
minus over 1 over 2 lambda

01:01:01.730 --> 01:01:06.370
and x2 is equal to
minus 1 over 2 lambda.

01:01:06.370 --> 01:01:13.770
Now I plug x1, x2 in here, so
that I can solve for lambda.

01:01:13.770 --> 01:01:16.530
So when I plug into the
constraint, so what I get

01:01:16.530 --> 01:01:23.530
is 1 over 4 lambda squared
plus 1 over 4 lambda squared

01:01:23.530 --> 01:01:32.400
equals 2, which implies 2
over 4 lambda squared equal 2.

01:01:32.400 --> 01:01:34.200
So this goes away.

01:01:34.200 --> 01:01:40.940
So lambda is equal
to plus minus 1/2.

01:01:46.760 --> 01:01:53.020
And then I go back here
and what I get is--

01:01:59.880 --> 01:02:14.000
so if lambda is equal to 12,
then x1 is equal to minus 1

01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:16.640
and x2 is equal to minus 1.

01:02:16.640 --> 01:02:20.160
And if lambda is
equal to minus 1/2,

01:02:20.160 --> 01:02:27.150
then x1 is equal to 1
and x2 is equal to 1,

01:02:27.150 --> 01:02:29.810
for which value the
function achieves--

01:02:29.810 --> 01:02:35.150
for which pair the
function achieves, sorry,

01:02:35.150 --> 01:02:37.790
lower value here.

01:02:37.790 --> 01:02:39.330
So that's a minimum.

01:02:45.430 --> 01:02:48.392
Actually, the Lagrange
multiplier does have value,

01:02:48.392 --> 01:02:50.350
but at least for the way
I'm presenting it here

01:02:50.350 --> 01:02:53.610
is just a means to an end.

01:02:53.610 --> 01:02:56.110
So we use it to solve
for these equations.

01:02:56.110 --> 01:02:58.670
But then once we get
to our local minima,

01:02:58.670 --> 01:03:01.685
we just throw it away.

01:03:01.685 --> 01:03:02.810
So it's just the mechanics.

01:03:02.810 --> 01:03:04.643
Now I'm going to give
you a bit of intuition

01:03:04.643 --> 01:03:06.030
about how this works.

01:03:06.030 --> 01:03:08.210
So think about
gradient equal to 0.

01:03:08.210 --> 01:03:10.830
But now there is this
additional sophistication

01:03:10.830 --> 01:03:14.100
where you also have to account
for the constraint gradients.

01:03:14.100 --> 01:03:16.350
You do that through the
notion of Lagrange multipliers

01:03:16.350 --> 01:03:19.990
that are additional
variables that you solve for.

01:03:19.990 --> 01:03:24.330
The good news, though, is that
between the Lagrange multiplier

01:03:24.330 --> 01:03:27.270
theorem and the
equality constraints,

01:03:27.270 --> 01:03:30.110
you have the same number
of variables as equations,

01:03:30.110 --> 01:03:32.198
so you can always
solve for all of that.

01:03:32.198 --> 01:03:34.490
Of course, it can be challenging
because these might be

01:03:34.490 --> 01:03:38.610
nonlinear algebraic equations.

01:03:38.610 --> 01:03:40.970
Let's look graphically
at what's happening here.

01:03:40.970 --> 01:03:43.210
And I'll come back
to this picture.

01:03:43.210 --> 01:03:48.970
So this is your feasible set.

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:53.590
And these are the level set
curves for your cost function.

01:03:53.590 --> 01:04:02.130
Remember that the cost
function is f, x1 plus x2.

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:12.970
So x1 plus x2 equals c defines
a line in x1, x2 space, namely

01:04:12.970 --> 01:04:17.410
the line x2 equal c minus x1.

01:04:17.410 --> 01:04:20.770
So the one that goes
through the origin

01:04:20.770 --> 01:04:23.440
will be x2 equal minus x1.

01:04:23.440 --> 01:04:26.820
As I go in this direction, I
actually have increasing values.

01:04:26.820 --> 01:04:32.540
So this would be x1 plus x2
equal 3 or 4, 5, and so on.

01:04:32.540 --> 01:04:36.800
So from this interpretation,
and you'll see later

01:04:36.800 --> 01:04:40.200
in a second what is
this useful, remember

01:04:40.200 --> 01:04:44.700
that the optimal point, as we
found it, is minus 1, minus 1.

01:04:44.700 --> 01:04:45.880
So that's the minimum.

01:04:45.880 --> 01:04:48.760
Which makes sense because
these are the level set.

01:04:48.760 --> 01:04:54.720
And we want to try to pick the
level set curve with the lowest

01:04:54.720 --> 01:04:59.220
possible value, but it still
intersects the feasible set.

01:04:59.220 --> 01:05:01.280
So as you move, you
move, you move until you

01:05:01.280 --> 01:05:03.440
get here the point of tangency.

01:05:03.440 --> 01:05:08.720
Here there is no
point x1 and x2 that

01:05:08.720 --> 01:05:11.800
will be feasible with
respect to the circle.

01:05:11.800 --> 01:05:14.600
The objective would
actually get a lower value,

01:05:14.600 --> 01:05:17.080
but is not allowed.

01:05:17.080 --> 01:05:18.060
Does that make sense?

01:05:20.990 --> 01:05:22.228
Maybe.

01:05:22.228 --> 01:05:24.270
Think a little bit about
this geometric intuition

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:27.070
because it's important.

01:05:27.070 --> 01:05:29.230
So again, this is
the constraint set.

01:05:29.230 --> 01:05:31.830
These are the level set curves.

01:05:31.830 --> 01:05:35.350
And these will be
the set of points

01:05:35.350 --> 01:05:38.990
such as x1 plus x2 is
equal 5, x1 plus x2

01:05:38.990 --> 01:05:42.330
is equal 3, x1 plus x2 equals 0.

01:05:42.330 --> 01:05:44.350
That will be this curve here.

01:05:44.350 --> 01:05:49.310
And here I have two points
for which x1 plus x2 equals 0,

01:05:49.310 --> 01:05:53.550
the one here and the one here.

01:05:53.550 --> 01:05:54.870
I can go even further.

01:05:54.870 --> 01:06:00.090
So x1 plus x2 equals minus 1
and so on until I get here,

01:06:00.090 --> 01:06:02.790
which is x1 plus
x2 equal minus 2.

01:06:02.790 --> 01:06:04.210
And I have an intersection here.

01:06:04.210 --> 01:06:06.750
So there is at least one point
that achieved that value.

01:06:06.750 --> 01:06:11.270
If I go further down, then
there is no point that-- yeah.

01:06:11.270 --> 01:06:13.230
Is there a geometric
interpretation

01:06:13.230 --> 01:06:14.730
of why we have two lambdas?

01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:16.510
Is that the max/min?

01:06:16.510 --> 01:06:21.020
Yes, because remember that this
is just a necessary condition

01:06:21.020 --> 01:06:22.120
for a stationary point.

01:06:22.120 --> 01:06:23.980
So it's not
differentiating between it

01:06:23.980 --> 01:06:25.660
being a maximum or a minimum.

01:06:25.660 --> 01:06:28.420
And in fact, if you plug
in at the other lambda,

01:06:28.420 --> 01:06:29.620
you get a maximum here.

01:06:29.620 --> 01:06:32.060
If we have lambda
equal to 0, would that

01:06:32.060 --> 01:06:38.460
mean that the constraint was not
important for the [INAUDIBLE]?

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:38.960
No.

01:06:38.960 --> 01:06:42.430
I mean, the constraint is always
important for the minimization

01:06:42.430 --> 01:06:44.180
because you're only
allowed to pick points

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:46.500
from the feasible set.

01:06:46.500 --> 01:06:49.460
Lambda equal 0,
what does that mean?

01:06:49.460 --> 01:06:55.660
So lambda equals 0-- remember
that here, the condition is not

01:06:55.660 --> 01:06:57.900
in terms of equality
constraints,

01:06:57.900 --> 01:07:02.260
is in terms of the
constraint gradients.

01:07:02.260 --> 01:07:04.900
So that's why lambda
equals 0 per se

01:07:04.900 --> 01:07:07.940
doesn't tell you immediately
a condition on the equality

01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:09.620
constraint per se
because [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:09.620 --> 01:07:12.220
the gradient of the constraint.

01:07:12.220 --> 01:07:20.650
But related to that, let's try
to be a bit more of intuition.

01:07:20.650 --> 01:07:23.110
And essentially, there are
two ways, at least in my mind,

01:07:23.110 --> 01:07:30.410
to interpret the Lagrange
multiplier theorem.

01:07:30.410 --> 01:07:47.050
So let's look at a
simple case where

01:07:47.050 --> 01:07:50.270
you have only one constraint.

01:07:50.270 --> 01:07:50.770
Yeah.

01:07:58.050 --> 01:08:02.530
I just, for simplicity,
avoided the parentheses x.

01:08:02.530 --> 01:08:04.170
So what the Lagrange
multiplier theorem

01:08:04.170 --> 01:08:07.890
is telling us is that
the gradient of f

01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:11.690
belongs to the subspace spanned
by the constraint gradients

01:08:11.690 --> 01:08:12.690
at x.

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:14.690
That is, the constraint
solution will

01:08:14.690 --> 01:08:18.550
be at the point of tangency
of the constrained cost curves

01:08:18.550 --> 01:08:20.670
and the constraint function.

01:08:20.670 --> 01:08:21.649
What does that mean?

01:08:21.649 --> 01:08:26.149
So here, what the Lagrange
multiplier theorem

01:08:26.149 --> 01:08:28.470
is saying is that
the gradient of f

01:08:28.470 --> 01:08:32.609
is equal to the gradient-- are
the candidate for optimality,

01:08:32.609 --> 01:08:34.950
the gradient of f is
equal to the gradient

01:08:34.950 --> 01:08:37.830
of h times a scalar.

01:08:37.830 --> 01:08:40.910
So basically, they
are collinear.

01:08:40.910 --> 01:08:47.029
But remember that the gradients
are orthogonal to the level set

01:08:47.029 --> 01:08:53.750
curves, which means that if the
gradient of the level set curve

01:08:53.750 --> 01:08:57.390
refer to the constraint
set, the gradient

01:08:57.390 --> 01:09:00.095
to the objective
function are collinear.

01:09:00.095 --> 01:09:01.470
It means that
those two level set

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:04.870
curves are tangent
to each other, which

01:09:04.870 --> 01:09:07.609
is really what you have here.

01:09:12.109 --> 01:09:14.290
So this is the situation.

01:09:14.290 --> 01:09:18.060
So we have the circle,
which is the feasible set.

01:09:18.060 --> 01:09:22.680
Here we have the point
minus 1, minus 1.

01:09:25.380 --> 01:09:34.460
The gradient was-- the gradient
of the objective function

01:09:34.460 --> 01:09:36.939
was 1, 1.

01:09:36.939 --> 01:09:45.420
And the gradient of h here
is equal to minus 2, minus 2.

01:09:45.420 --> 01:09:48.040
So you see that the gradient
of the objective function

01:09:48.040 --> 01:09:50.580
and the gradient of
the constraint set

01:09:50.580 --> 01:09:57.620
are collinear, which means that
the constraint set and the level

01:09:57.620 --> 01:09:59.540
set curve for the
objective function

01:09:59.540 --> 01:10:03.220
are tangent to each other,
which notionally captures

01:10:03.220 --> 01:10:05.300
the fact that to
minimize, you're

01:10:05.300 --> 01:10:09.540
trying to push your level set
curve for the objective function

01:10:09.540 --> 01:10:14.330
as much as possible towards
some situation whereby

01:10:14.330 --> 01:10:17.050
if you push further, you don't
have intersection anymore

01:10:17.050 --> 01:10:19.050
with your constraint set.

01:10:19.050 --> 01:10:22.370
If you could push it even
farther, then you would do so.

01:10:22.370 --> 01:10:27.530
Tangency is basically the
geometric notion that captures

01:10:27.530 --> 01:10:30.490
the fact that you
cannot push it further.

01:10:30.490 --> 01:10:33.890
Some intuition here, but it's
actually correct intuition.

01:10:33.890 --> 01:10:34.670
Makes sense?

01:10:37.365 --> 01:10:38.990
So that's the more
geometric intuition.

01:10:38.990 --> 01:10:42.010
The other way that I like to
think about Lagrange multipliers

01:10:42.010 --> 01:10:43.330
is as follows.

01:10:43.330 --> 01:10:46.350
They might sound a
little bit cryptic,

01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:50.730
but actually, it's even
clearer in my opinion.

01:10:50.730 --> 01:10:52.390
So the cost gradient.

01:10:52.390 --> 01:10:54.890
So what the Lagrange
multiplier theorem is saying,

01:10:54.890 --> 01:10:59.370
remember, is saying that
the gradient of the cost

01:10:59.370 --> 01:11:08.450
function at a local minimum
is a linear combination

01:11:08.450 --> 01:11:13.480
of the gradient of
the constraints.

01:11:13.480 --> 01:11:16.660
So what that implies, and
we'll see in a second.

01:11:16.660 --> 01:11:19.720
So first, let me state it
and then I'll explain it.

01:11:19.720 --> 01:11:24.520
What that implies is that the
cost gradient or gradient of f

01:11:24.520 --> 01:11:29.060
is orthogonal to the subspace
of a first order feasible

01:11:29.060 --> 01:11:30.240
variations.

01:11:30.240 --> 01:11:31.920
We define the
feasible variations

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:36.160
as those variations that are
orthogonal to the constraint

01:11:36.160 --> 01:11:38.280
gradients.

01:11:38.280 --> 01:11:40.060
So, what is it saying?

01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:47.800
So v of x star is the
space of displacements

01:11:47.800 --> 01:11:51.040
that are allowable up to
first-order, meaning that they

01:11:51.040 --> 01:11:53.360
lead you to a point that
is still feasible up

01:11:53.360 --> 01:11:54.720
to first-order.

01:11:54.720 --> 01:12:02.040
Specifically h of
x star plus delta

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:08.200
x is equal to h of
x star plus gradient

01:12:08.200 --> 01:12:14.200
of h times evaluated
x star times delta x.

01:12:16.980 --> 01:12:21.540
So if you choose
delta x from this set,

01:12:21.540 --> 01:12:26.420
then this term is equal to 0.

01:12:26.420 --> 01:12:28.420
This term is also
equal to 0 because we

01:12:28.420 --> 01:12:29.960
are assuming that
x star is feasible

01:12:29.960 --> 01:12:32.820
since it's at a local minimum,
which means that this is also

01:12:32.820 --> 01:12:34.620
equal to 0.

01:12:34.620 --> 01:12:36.660
So all this is to
say that if you

01:12:36.660 --> 01:12:43.020
move along these displacements,
you are moving away from x star

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:46.500
to new points that, at
least up to first-m order,

01:12:46.500 --> 01:12:49.640
satisfy the constraints.

01:12:54.140 --> 01:13:10.950
But remember, but
then, if that is true,

01:13:10.950 --> 01:13:17.490
then delta f of x star
times delta x is equal to 0,

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:20.050
thanks to the Lagrange
multiplier theorem.

01:13:20.050 --> 01:13:23.530
Since the gradient of f is
a linear combination of all

01:13:23.530 --> 01:13:27.330
these constraint
gradients, and the delta x

01:13:27.330 --> 01:13:29.090
is fixed in such a
way that the scalar

01:13:29.090 --> 01:13:31.673
product between delta x, each
one of these constraint gradient

01:13:31.673 --> 01:13:34.810
is equal to 0, then I'm just--

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:39.730
if I replace with the lambda
i's gradient of a choice,

01:13:39.730 --> 01:13:42.010
all this goes to 0.

01:13:42.010 --> 01:13:45.970
So what I'm saying here is
that you can view the Lagrange

01:13:45.970 --> 01:13:49.370
multiplier theorem as
essentially embodying

01:13:49.370 --> 01:13:54.610
the conditions whereby
the gradient of f

01:13:54.610 --> 01:13:57.770
has to be orthogonal to
the space of feasible

01:13:57.770 --> 01:14:00.250
variations, which
is exactly what we

01:14:00.250 --> 01:14:03.850
had in the case of unconstrained
optimization, if you remember.

01:14:03.850 --> 01:14:06.650
And then we said that
delta x can be anything.

01:14:06.650 --> 01:14:10.720
And that leads to the
condition gradient equal to 0.

01:14:10.720 --> 01:14:12.780
Here delta x cannot be anything.

01:14:12.780 --> 01:14:16.680
Delta x has to be taken
from this set here.

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:18.900
And so the condition is
a bit more sophisticated.

01:14:18.900 --> 01:14:21.920
It's saying that the gradient
of f, the most we can say

01:14:21.920 --> 01:14:26.080
has to be orthogonal
to all this delta x.

01:14:26.080 --> 01:14:29.680
I mean, my point here
is that what we're doing

01:14:29.680 --> 01:14:33.320
is just a more sophisticated
way of what we did before

01:14:33.320 --> 01:14:36.343
with unconstrained optimization.

01:14:36.343 --> 01:14:38.760
But now we have to be more
sophisticated in the sense that

01:14:38.760 --> 01:14:41.960
we cannot reason about all
possible displacements,

01:14:41.960 --> 01:14:45.360
only those that satisfy
the equality constraints up

01:14:45.360 --> 01:14:48.560
to first-order.

01:14:48.560 --> 01:14:51.860
So this leads us to
the key result here.

01:14:51.860 --> 01:14:55.160
And we will see this result
showing up again, of course,

01:14:55.160 --> 01:14:58.320
with some modifications
in optimal control, which

01:14:58.320 --> 01:15:00.800
is a necessary optimality
condition for equality

01:15:00.800 --> 01:15:02.160
constraint problems.

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:05.520
So let's assume that x star
is a local minimum of f

01:15:05.520 --> 01:15:07.910
subject to h of x equals 0.

01:15:07.910 --> 01:15:10.790
And assume, and that's
a technical condition,

01:15:10.790 --> 01:15:14.888
that the constraint gradients
are linearly independent.

01:15:14.888 --> 01:15:16.430
Turns out that if
it is not the case,

01:15:16.430 --> 01:15:19.830
actually is no longer a
necessary optimality condition.

01:15:19.830 --> 01:15:23.510
I don't want to elaborate
too much on this condition.

01:15:23.510 --> 01:15:27.210
Main point is that always check
that for a candidate optimality,

01:15:27.210 --> 01:15:29.630
the constraint gradients
are linearly independent.

01:15:29.630 --> 01:15:32.790
Then there exists a unique
vector lambda 1, lambda m,

01:15:32.790 --> 01:15:36.150
called a Lagrange multiplier
vector, such as the gradient

01:15:36.150 --> 01:15:38.810
of f is a linear combination
of the constraint gradients.

01:15:44.150 --> 01:15:47.030
So feasible vector for which
the constraint gradients

01:15:47.030 --> 01:15:49.990
are linearly independent
is called regular.

01:15:49.990 --> 01:15:52.750
How do we prove this theorem
is actually a little bit

01:15:52.750 --> 01:15:56.550
convoluted, but essentially, can
be proved in a couple of ways.

01:15:56.550 --> 01:16:00.110
First, again, as we said
before, penalty approach.

01:16:00.110 --> 01:16:02.530
We move the constraints
to the cost function.

01:16:02.530 --> 01:16:05.660
We penalize deviations
from feasibility.

01:16:05.660 --> 01:16:12.380
And then we make
a limit argument

01:16:12.380 --> 01:16:14.860
to derive the Lagrange
multiplier theorem,

01:16:14.860 --> 01:16:18.880
or maybe a little bit
more direct and intuitive,

01:16:18.880 --> 01:16:20.650
but actually it's a bit--

01:16:20.650 --> 01:16:23.220
not convoluted, but it's
a little bit more annoying

01:16:23.220 --> 01:16:24.740
in terms of bookkeeping.

01:16:24.740 --> 01:16:28.140
So what you do is that you
have n variables and m equality

01:16:28.140 --> 01:16:29.380
constraints.

01:16:29.380 --> 01:16:32.420
So you express m
of the variables

01:16:32.420 --> 01:16:35.420
in terms of the
remaining n minus m.

01:16:35.420 --> 01:16:39.900
And then you reduce the problem
to an unconstrained optimization

01:16:39.900 --> 01:16:42.780
problem.

01:16:42.780 --> 01:16:44.980
And then you do three
pages of analysis

01:16:44.980 --> 01:16:47.660
by just saying what the
intuition comes from.

01:16:47.660 --> 01:16:50.020
An excellent book, if you're
interested in nonlinear

01:16:50.020 --> 01:16:53.420
optimization is the book of
Dimitri Bertsekas, Nonlinear

01:16:53.420 --> 01:16:55.460
Optimization.

01:16:55.460 --> 01:16:58.500
If you want to learn more
about convergence rates

01:16:58.500 --> 01:17:01.460
for gradient descent methods or
proofs of Lagrange multiplier

01:17:01.460 --> 01:17:05.440
theorems, explore a little bit
more these technical conditions

01:17:05.440 --> 01:17:06.060
and so on.

01:17:09.440 --> 01:17:11.420
Mnemonically, and
this is, again,

01:17:11.420 --> 01:17:14.240
something that will come back
in optimal control, what you

01:17:14.240 --> 01:17:17.200
do with linearly, sorry,
equality constraint problems

01:17:17.200 --> 01:17:20.280
is to form the so-called
Lagrangian function.

01:17:20.280 --> 01:17:21.920
The Lagrangian
function is a function

01:17:21.920 --> 01:17:28.240
that comprises your objective
plus you now lift your equality

01:17:28.240 --> 01:17:32.720
constraint into the
cost and you scale down

01:17:32.720 --> 01:17:34.760
by multiplying
this lambda, which

01:17:34.760 --> 01:17:37.880
are additional variables
you have to find.

01:17:37.880 --> 01:17:40.000
And then you just
read the problem

01:17:40.000 --> 01:17:43.320
as an unconstrained optimization
problem with respect

01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:46.560
to this augmented cost function.

01:17:46.560 --> 01:17:49.360
So then the necessary
optimality condition

01:17:49.360 --> 01:17:51.360
with respect to the
Lagrangian becomes

01:17:51.360 --> 01:17:55.680
set the Lagrangian L equal
to 0, as if the problem

01:17:55.680 --> 01:17:57.400
was unconstrained.

01:17:57.400 --> 01:18:00.560
And what you get when you
take the gradient with respect

01:18:00.560 --> 01:18:05.070
to the x variables, you get
exactly the Lagrange multiplier

01:18:05.070 --> 01:18:05.670
theorem.

01:18:05.670 --> 01:18:08.590
So gradient of f plus sum
of lambda x gradient of hi

01:18:08.590 --> 01:18:10.030
is equal to 0.

01:18:10.030 --> 01:18:12.670
When you take the gradient
with respect to the lambdas,

01:18:12.670 --> 01:18:15.390
you get the different
condition h1 equals 0, h2

01:18:15.390 --> 01:18:17.990
equals 0, and so
on and so forth.

01:18:17.990 --> 01:18:21.510
Notably, the gradient
with respect to x

01:18:21.510 --> 01:18:24.030
leads to a set of n equations.

01:18:24.030 --> 01:18:25.790
The gradient with
respect to lambda

01:18:25.790 --> 01:18:28.590
leads to a set of m equations.

01:18:28.590 --> 01:18:32.550
And we have n plus m variables.

01:18:32.550 --> 01:18:36.850
So we have m plus m variables
over n plus m equations.

01:18:36.850 --> 01:18:38.350
So you can solve for that.

01:18:38.350 --> 01:18:40.910
So that's what I wanted to say
about the equality constraint

01:18:40.910 --> 01:18:41.710
problems.

01:18:41.710 --> 01:18:44.010
Next time, we'll
have a quick look,

01:18:44.010 --> 01:18:47.630
and this will be quite brief,
at the inequality constraint

01:18:47.630 --> 01:18:51.070
case which will lead you to
the so-called [INAUDIBLE]

01:18:51.070 --> 01:18:52.090
conditions.

01:18:52.090 --> 01:18:54.270
And then we'll move
into our first topic

01:18:54.270 --> 01:18:56.820
related to optimal control.

